# $project

A fleet of [AgentFrame](https://github.com/malcmiller/agentframe) agents, each
served over A2A as its own process. Created by `agentframe new fleet`; the code
is yours to edit.

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
$agent_rows| `agentframe.yaml` | The agents `agentframe dev` starts, in order, with their ports. |
| `agents.yaml` | A registry of the specialists, for agents you start yourself. |
| `tests/` | Tests that never call a model provider or start a server. |

Each specialist's skill is tagged `content`. Its placeholder body marks the
text it receives; replace it with real work and describe that work in the
skill's docstring, which callers (and planning models) read.
$planner_section
## Set up and check

```shell
uv sync
uv run ruff format --check
uv run ruff check
uv run mypy
uv run pytest
```

## Run

`agentframe dev` starts every agent in `agentframe.yaml` on `127.0.0.1`,
generates one development API key per agent, and gives each agent a registry
of the others. Set your own key to call them:

```shell
${dev_command}```

Call an agent's skill with the header `X-API-Key: dev-only-key`. `agents.yaml`
lists the same specialists for agents you start yourself with
`agentframe run`; `agentframe dev` writes its own registry instead.

## Before production

- The keys from `dev_settings()` are **development placeholders**:
  `dev_settings()` refuses to run without `AGENTFRAME_DEV=1`. Use real
  authentication, such as Microsoft Entra ID:
  `HostConfig(auth=EntraIDAuth(tenant_id=..., audience=...))` with the `entra`
  extra, and a registry of the deployed agents' HTTPS card URLs.
- Review each agent's `limits`, which bound every run.
- `agentframe compose` writes a development `compose.yaml` from
  `agentframe.yaml`, to run the agents as containers.
